smtdif: how hard an SMT formula is, judged by its shape
========================================================

smtdif predicts what an SMT solver will do with a formula, and how long it
will take, from the shape of the formula alone: 67 counts read off its syntax
before any solving starts.

The finding is that the shape carries the answer only as far as the family of
benchmarks goes.  With the instances dealt at random among training and test,
a random forest predicts the log10 solve time of cvc5 with a mean absolute
error of 0.16 over 18,271 formulas, and Spearman 0.96.  With every family kept
whole in one part, no learner beats the median of the training times.  The
features order the formulas of a family and recognize where a formula came
from, but do not set the time of a family the model never saw: one solved
instance of the new family recovers most of what was lost.


The programs
------------

Six filters over text, each doing one thing, written in Rust with no framework
and two crates, to undo zstd and gzip.  Each reads the file named after its
flags, or else the standard input, and writes tables of tab separated text.

  smtfeat  the features of every formula fed to it: a formula, or a tar
           archive of them, zstd compressed or not, never unpacked on disk
  smtdata  the dataset: the competition results of one solver joined with the
           features of their formulas
  smtstat  what the dataset holds, in tables
  smtfit   every family of learners under cross validation, under both splits
           and all three tasks
  smtcal   what a few instances of a family new to the model are worth
  smtimp   what the features are worth: by permutation, and by ablation of
           groups of them

The learners are written here too, on one interface: the median and the
majority as the guesses to beat, ridge, one logistic fit a class, the k
nearest neighbors, gaussian naive Bayes, a CART tree, a random forest,
gradient boosting, and a support vector machine over a radial kernel.  Each
was checked against scikit-learn on the same rows with the same settings:
ridge agrees to the last digit, and the others stay within 0.02 of error on
the time.


Building and running
--------------------

Needs cargo, make and a POSIX shell with curl and md5sum.

  make          the six programs, under target/release
  make test     the 53 tests of every stage
  make dataset  the dataset, out of the archives and the competition results
  make stats    what the dataset holds, in out/stat.txt
  make fit      every experiment, its tables in out/
  make calib    the calibration of the predictions by family, out/calib.tsv
  make worth    what the features are worth, out/imp.tsv and out/ablate.tsv

The first run fetches 1.9 GB from Zenodo and GitHub (get.sh, eight ranges at
a time; make checks the md5 of each archive), extracts the features in a few
minutes, and then spends about an hour on twenty processors in smtfit, most
of it in the support vector machines.  Every stage writes files that the next
stage reads, and make runs a stage again only when what it reads is newer
than what it wrote:

  smtfeat QF_BV.tar.zst             > feat/QF_BV.tsv
  smtfeat QF_ABV.tar.zst            > feat/QF_ABV.tsv
  smtdata -s cvc5 -l QF_BV -l QF_ABV -f feat/QF_BV.tsv -f feat/QF_ABV.tsv \
          results-sq-2025.json.gz > dataset.tsv
  smtstat dataset.tsv               > out/stat.txt
  smtfit  -o out dataset.tsv        # writes time.tsv, outcome.tsv, pred.tsv
  smtcal  out/pred.tsv              > out/calib.tsv
  smtimp  -o out dataset.tsv        # writes imp.tsv, ablate.tsv

The data end in data/ and the tables in out/.  The seed 2026 decides every
split and every draw, and a run repeats: a second run reproduced all ten
tables byte for byte.

make clean removes out/ and the build.

On Windows the programs carry .exe; make knows the suffix from the system, or
else from the name of the cargo at hand.  make runs there under WSL or any
POSIX shell, with the directory of the Windows cargo on the PATH and
CARGO=cargo.exe.  The programs take their input by name because the standard
input does not always cross that boundary whole.


The data
--------

The formulas are the SMT-LIB release of 2025 (Zenodo, record 16740866), in the
two logics QF_BV and QF_ABV.  The times are the cvc5 results of SMT-COMP 2025,
single query track.  The dataset holds 18,271 benchmarks of 58 families: 9,833
sat, 7,442 unsat and 996 timeouts (5.5%: 962 by time, 34 by memory), with no
empty cell.

An instance is a benchmark.  The features fall in six groups: size (the bytes
and the counts of assertions, declarations, definitions, operators and
literals), the bitvector widths of the variables, the nesting depth of terms,
the multiplicative operators, the arrays, and the count of each of the other
operators.  The regression target is log10 of the wall clock time, a timeout
counted at twice the limit.  The classification target is the outcome among
sat, unsat and timeout.

The extractor was checked against the SMT-LIB catalog on 222 formulas of 22
families: size, assertions, declarations and depth agree on all, and so do all
2,701 counts of operators.


The protocol
------------

The rows are split 70, 15 and 15 into training, validation and test, twice:
at random, and by family, every family whole in one part.  Each family of
learners is fitted over a grid of settings by cross validation in five folds
over the training rows; the chosen setting is fitted again over all of them
and measured on validation and on test.  Validation chooses the learner, and
test measures it once.  Time is scored by mean absolute error and Spearman
correlation in the logarithm; the outcome by accuracy, mean F1 and the F1 of
the timeouts.


What came of it
---------------

                        time, MAE        Spearman     outcome, mean F1
  random split, forest   0.159            0.958        0.814
  random split, kNN      0.164            0.941        0.831
  by family, median      0.765            0.000        0.298
  by family, best        1.063 (ridge)    0.652        0.292 (Bayes)

Under the split by family, 63% of the test rows are one family,
20221012-MCMPC, which cvc5 solves in 0.37 s and the forest puts at 8 s.  With
one solved instance of each new family to set an offset, the forest falls from
1.24 to 0.54, and orders the formulas of a family it never saw with Spearman
0.69.  The features that carry the signal are size and the counts of
operators; the multiplicative operators and the depth add nothing that the
others lack.


The layout
----------

  src/         the library: the readers (buf, sexp, tar, stream, json, table),
               the features (feat), the learners (lin, knn, bayes, tree,
               forest, svm), the experiment (split, prep, exp), and the rest
  src/bin/     the six programs
  man/1/       their manual pages
  data/        the archives, the results and the tables out of them
  out/         the tables of the experiments

data/ and out/ are written by make, not source.  Each program documents
itself: man ./man/1/smtfeat, and so on.

The code is under the ISC license (LICENSE).  The benchmarks and the results
belong to SMT-LIB and SMT-COMP, under their own licenses.
